<p class="heritage-longread__dek">A family-history narrative for Patience, Daniel, and Faith Cheesman &mdash; the children in whom the islands finally come ashore.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-crossing-of-the-water"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Prologue</span>The crossing of the water</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Lewis · South Uist · Moray → Saskatchewan → Alberta · c. 1832 to today</p><p>The Outer Hebrides had a phrase for the hardest journey of all &mdash; not a walk to the next township or a row across a sea-loch for peat, but the journey from which there is usually no return: <em>a&rsquo; dol thar a&rsquo; chuain</em>, the crossing of the water. When an island family said it in the 1880s, they meant that a son, a daughter, or a whole household was about to climb into the dark of a steerage hold and trade the only world they had known &mdash; the byre, the burn, the headstones of every grandparent back to the edge of memory &mdash; for a rumour of land on the far side of an ocean.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>A&rsquo; dol thar a&rsquo; chuain &mdash; the crossing of the water.</p></aside><p>This is the story of three families who made that crossing. They were islanders and Highlanders, Free Presbyterians who would not bow and Roman Catholics who would not convert, out of three different corners of the Gàidhealtachd &mdash; the Gaelic-speaking world &mdash; who under any ordinary turning of history would never have met. A man from the Lochs of Lewis had no reason to marry a woman from the machair of South Uist; a coastal Moray family had no obvious thread to either. Yet all three streams ran out of the islands in the same terrible decade, poured into the same few square miles of southeastern Saskatchewan, and there &mdash; against the grain of three centuries of Highland history &mdash; they joined.</p><p>They joined in a marriage that, if the island geography holds, crossed one of the hardest lines in the Hebrides. Out of it came a son, a granddaughter, and a line that runs straight down the twentieth century and arrives &mdash; by way of Edmonton, Calgary, and at last Grande Prairie &mdash; at three children alive in Alberta today.</p><p>The Hebrideans had a word for the virtue this story is really about: <em>buanseasmhachd</em>. The dictionary says &ldquo;steadfastness,&rdquo; but the truest sense is the crofter&rsquo;s &mdash; holding your ground when the ground itself is being pulled out from under you. It is what gets a family of a dozen children through a Saskatchewan winter in a house built of turf. These three families had it, and needed every grain. This is the record of where they spent it, and of what came through on the far side.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-world-that-cleared-them-out-the-highlands-an"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter One</span>The world that cleared them out &mdash; the Highlands and Islands, c. 1780&ndash;1850</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">c. 1780&ndash;1850 · the Highland Clearances &amp; the Famine</p><p>To understand why a McIver left Lewis, a Campbell left South Uist, and a Cameron left Moray, you have to understand a catastrophe with a deceptively gentle name. Historians call it the Highland Clearances; the people who lived through it called it <em>Fuadach nan Gàidheal</em> &mdash; the driving-out of the Gaels. It was the deliberate dismantling of an entire way of life across the north and west of Scotland, and it sits behind every page of this story.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/mciver/mciver-clearances-eviction.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A Highland township being cleared, a cottage roof burning as a family walks away" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Highland township cleared &mdash; roof-timbers burning, a family turned out onto the road. The world that drove the McIvers, Campbells, and Camerons out. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><p>For centuries the Gaelic Highlands and Hebrides had been organised around the clan and the <em>baile</em>, the township &mdash; families who held land in common and worked it together, grew oats and barley and potatoes in thin acid soil, grazed cattle on the high pastures, cut peat, and lived close-packed in low stone-and-turf houses. It was hard and often hungry, but it was theirs. After the Jacobite defeat at Culloden in 1745, the state set out to break the clan system, and the chiefs themselves turned from patriarchs into landlords. A chief&rsquo;s status had been measured in fighting men; now it was measured in rent &mdash; and the most reliable rent came not from people but from sheep.</p><p>The great Cheviot flocks could turn empty hills into money on a scale mixed farming never could, provided the hills were empty. So across roughly seventy years the people were cleared &mdash; sometimes with paper, rents raised past any crofter&rsquo;s reach; sometimes with fire, the roof-timbers burned so the evicted could not creep back. They were pushed off the fertile inland straths onto narrow coastal crofts deliberately made too small to live on, so the people would <em>have</em> to take wage-work in kelp, the fisheries, or eventually a ship.</p><p>Then, in 1846, the potato &mdash; the one crop the crofting poor could not do without &mdash; began to rot in the ground, as it was rotting in Ireland. The Highland Potato Famine was less murderous than the Irish one, but it was long: failure after failure into the 1850s, in regions where three-quarters of the people had depended on the potato. Landlords faced with starving tenants who could pay no rent increasingly reached the same conclusion &mdash; the cheapest thing to do with these people was to ship them somewhere else. &ldquo;Assisted emigration&rdquo; became a Highland industry. Some went willingly, gambling everything on land of their own; some were marched aboard at the factor&rsquo;s word, the Gaelic psalms rising off the decks as the only coastline they had ever known dropped below the horizon.</p><p>Donald McIver was born into the tail end of this, on Lewis around 1850 &mdash; a famine-era child, in a world where the question was no longer <em>whether</em> the family would leave but <em>when</em>. Elizabeth &ldquo;Lizzie&rdquo; Campbell was born on South Uist around 1860, into a Catholic crofting community on one of the hardest-cleared islands of all. And a generation earlier, in the coastal parish of Urquhart on the Moray Firth, Catherine Cameron was born around 1844. None of the three knew the others. But the wind that would blow them all to the same prairie was already up.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-james-alexander-campbell-1832-1920-the-southern-"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Two</span>James Alexander Campbell (1832&ndash;1920) &mdash; the southern isle and the long patience of the cleared</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1832&ndash;1920 · South Uist → Wapella, Saskatchewan</p><p>We begin the documented line with the oldest person the records let us see plainly: <strong>James Alexander Campbell</strong>, born <strong>1 November 1832</strong> on <strong>South Uist</strong>. He is the great-great-great-grandfather of the children this story is for, and he lived eighty-seven years across two centuries and two continents.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/mciver/mciver-blackhouse-croft.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A Hebridean blackhouse with a thatched roof, a crofting family, and cattle" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A Hebridean blackhouse &mdash; the stone-and-thatch crofting life of the islands the Campbells and McIvers were born to. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><p>South Uist in 1832 was a place apart even within the Hebrides. Most of the Outer Isles had gone fiercely Protestant; but South Uist, with Barra and parts of Benbecula, stayed Catholic through the whole Reformation &mdash; an island of the old faith ringed by the new. To be a Campbell of South Uist was to be a Gaelic-speaking Catholic crofter on land owned by a Protestant aristocracy that increasingly regarded both your religion and your presence as inconvenient. The family&rsquo;s likely townships are the low <em>machair</em> ground &mdash; Iochdar in the north, or Dalibrog and Askernish toward the south &mdash; the shell-sand grassland that was the island&rsquo;s one real treasure.</p><p>James came of age through the worst of it: thirteen or fourteen when the potato failed in 1846, an adult in the famine decade under Colonel John Gordon of Cluny, who had bought South Uist, Benbecula, and Barra together and cleared tenants by the shipload &mdash; there are accounts from the early 1850s of families chased down and forced aboard emigrant vessels. James survived all of it, married a woman named <strong>Catherine Cameron</strong>, and stayed &mdash; for a while. What is remarkable is how long he held on: he was past fifty before he finally crossed the water, with a family grown half to adulthood around him. That is the <em>buanseasmhachd</em> of the man &mdash; he did not leave until staying had become impossible, and even then he left for the one thing the islands could never give a Catholic crofter: land of his own. We follow him onto the ship in Chapter Four.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-catherine-cameron-b-c-1844-the-woman-from-the-sp"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Three</span>Catherine Cameron (b. c. 1844) &mdash; the woman from the Spey-mouth, and the honest edge of the record</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">b. c. 1844 · Urquhart, Moray (Spey Bay)</p><p>The third stream comes from the east, off the Moray Firth, and it comes with a caution this story will honour.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/mciver/mciver-moray-spey-coast.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A fishing village on the Moray Firth coast with boats drawn up and nets drying" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A fishing village on the Moray Firth near Spey Bay &mdash; the coast Catherine Cameron came from. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>We hold her name honestly and lightly.</p></aside><p><strong>Catherine Cameron</strong> was born, the family genealogy holds, around <strong>1844</strong>, in the parish of <strong>Urquhart in Moray</strong> &mdash; and here we must be exact, because there are two Urquharts in Scotland and confusing them has misled more than one family historian. The famous one is on Loch Ness, with its ruined castle. <em>This</em> is not that one. This Urquhart is a coastal parish on the south shore of the Moray Firth, near Spey Bay, where the River Spey empties into the North Sea. In the catalogue of Scottish parish registers it is <strong>parish number 144</strong>, whose Old Parish Registers run (with gaps) from 1647 to 1854 &mdash; so if the family date is right, a baptism for a Catherine Cameron around 1844 ought to lie waiting in those pages, not yet retrieved.</p><p>That last clause matters. Catherine is, at this writing, a <em>probable</em> ancestor rather than a proven one: her existence, her birth around 1844, her Moray origin, and her marriage to James Alexander Campbell all come down through the family tree &mdash; consistent and plausible, but not yet confirmed by the baptism register or a marriage certificate. The line is almost certainly real. The single sheet of parchment that would turn &ldquo;probable&rdquo; into &ldquo;verified&rdquo; has not yet been pulled, and this story will not pretend otherwise.</p><p>The Spey-mouth country in the 1840s was a borderland: westward lay the Gaelic Highlands, eastward the Lowland, Scots-speaking, Protestant farming and fishing coast. The Camerons are a Highland name &mdash; one of the great clans of Lochaber &mdash; and a Cameron family near Spey Bay sits exactly on that seam. The coast ran on herring and salmon; inland, the farms grew barley for the Speyside distilleries. It was, by Highland standards, a <em>prosperous</em> edge &mdash; part of why the Camerons did not flee in the famine the way the islanders did. Catherine&rsquo;s crossing, when it came, would come later and as part of someone else&rsquo;s story: bound up with the old Catholic of South Uist she married, and the children they carried to Canada together. She is the quietest figure here &mdash; a name, a place, a date, all honestly tagged not-yet-proven &mdash; but the Highland clan-name Cameron runs in three Alberta children because of her, and that is worth saying plainly.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-donald-george-mciver-1850-1932-the-man-from-the-"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Four</span>Donald George McIver (1850&ndash;1932) &mdash; the man from the moor of Lewis, and how he got to Saskatchewan</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1850&ndash;1932 · Lochs, Lewis → Saltcoats, Saskatchewan · immigrated 1883</p><p>Now the man whose surname the maternal line carries across the ocean: <strong>Donald George McIver</strong>, born <strong>1850</strong> in the parish of <strong>Lochs</strong>, Isle of Lewis. He was the son of <strong>Norman MacIver</strong> and <strong>Henrietta MacIver, born MacAulay</strong> &mdash; both Lewis people of the same moor. (McIver &mdash; in Gaelic <em>MacÌomhair</em>, &ldquo;son of Ivor&rdquo; &mdash; is one of the great surnames of Lewis, and Lochs one of its heartlands.)</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/mciver/mciver-emigrant-ship-1883.jpg' ); ?>" alt="An emigrant ship boarding at a Scottish quay, families carrying trunks aboard" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An emigrant ship boarding at a Scottish quay &mdash; the crossing Donald McIver made out of Lewis in 1883. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><p>The parish of Lochs takes up the wild, sea-pierced southeastern quarter of Lewis: black peat moor, low rock, and a coast so indented that almost nowhere is more than a mile or two from saltwater. Poor ground and proud people. Donald&rsquo;s Lewis was overwhelmingly Gaelic-speaking and overwhelmingly <strong>Free Presbyterian</strong> &mdash; a religious culture of extraordinary seriousness, built around the Sabbath and the Gaelic psalm sung in the long, unaccompanied style still called &ldquo;precenting the line.&rdquo; To be a McIver of Lochs was to be formed by that faith as much as by that ground, and Donald carried it across the world &mdash; which is what makes the marriage he eventually entered so quietly astonishing.</p><p>The line can be reached back a little further. A FamilySearch tree confirms Donald&rsquo;s father as <strong>Norman MacIver</strong> (c. 1811&ndash;1893), and a compiled clan genealogy carries it one more generation, to <strong>Neil McIver (c. 1779&ndash;c. 1820)</strong> and his wife <strong>Catherine MacArthur (1775&ndash;1862)</strong> &mdash; names reaching into the eighteenth-century Lewis of the runrig townships, before the Clearances. These earlier generations are tagged <em>inherited</em> &mdash; from a secondary compilation, not yet checked against Lewis parish registers &mdash; but they push the documented horizon back the better part of a century.</p><p>Donald was born into the long aftermath of clearance and famine, into a Lewis where the crofts were too small and the future pointed across the Atlantic. <strong>He immigrated in 1883.</strong> That year matters, because the story here meets two great organised emigration schemes &mdash; two emigrant fleets sailing for the same prairie within a year of each other, easy to confuse.</p><p>The first was the <strong>Cathcart Colony</strong>. In <strong>1883 and 1884</strong>, Lady Gordon Cathcart &mdash; who held vast Hebridean estates including much of South Uist and Benbecula &mdash; sponsored the emigration of nearly <strong>three hundred Hebridean crofters</strong> to southeastern Saskatchewan, settling them in the <strong>St. Andrew&rsquo;s and Benbecula Colonies</strong> near Moosomin and Wapella. Each family received a <strong>£100 loan</strong> against their homestead; they arrived to no houses, lodged in tents, and built their first dwellings of turf and log. In 1888 a church was founded for the Catholic among them &mdash; <strong>St. Andrew&rsquo;s parish at Wapella</strong>. This is the scheme that fits <strong>James Alexander Campbell</strong> of South Uist: the right island, the right years, the right destination. It has not yet been nailed to a passenger list, so the story holds it as <em>probable</em>.</p><p>The second, the <strong>Lothian Colony (1889)</strong>, brought Lewis crofters to the Stornoway and Kessock hamlets north of Saltcoats &mdash; and Donald&rsquo;s later homestead (NE 36-24-01-W2) sits in that colony&rsquo;s ground. But Donald arrived six years <em>before</em> it. He was not a Lothian settler; he was a pioneering Lewis emigrant who got there first, and he did not go straight to Saltcoats. He went to the <strong>Earlswood/Wapella area</strong> &mdash; the heart of Cathcart territory &mdash; and on <strong>13 February 1888</strong> married <strong>Elizabeth Ann &ldquo;Lizzie&rdquo; Campbell at Earlswood, Saskatchewan</strong>, the same Earlswood where Lizzie&rsquo;s father, James Alexander Campbell, would be buried in 1920. Donald had settled near the Campbells. Afterward he and Lizzie moved north to the Saltcoats district and took up the homestead quarter the Saskatchewan Archives still carries under his name.</p><p>So picture it: within about two years, two fleets of Gaels &mdash; Catholics of the southern isles bound for Wapella, Presbyterians of Lewis bound for Saltcoats &mdash; crossed the Atlantic, rode the new Canadian Pacific Railway west, and were set down within forty miles of one another on open prairie. They came from islands a hundred miles apart that had, in all the centuries before, almost nothing to do with each other. The land was not the land they had been promised: a short, savage growing season, killing winters, drought, prairie fire, and isolation on a scale no islander had known. The Saltcoats colonies, the records say flatly, <strong>had essentially failed by 1900</strong>, and the families scattered &mdash; many, in time, to Edmonton. But before they scattered, a McIver met a Campbell. And held.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-brother-the-family-lost-to-the-rebellion"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Interlude &mdash; George McIver (1856&ndash;1885)</span>The brother the family lost to the Rebellion</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1856&ndash;1885 · Lewis → Maple Creek, the North-West Rebellion</p><p>Donald did not cross the ocean alone, and not every McIver who came west lived to farm it. He had at least two brothers who also made the crossing &mdash; <strong>Roderick</strong> and <strong>George</strong> &mdash; and George&rsquo;s story is one the wider family has never let go. It was set down in a typed account read aloud on 17 June 1990, when McIver and MacIver descendants gathered to rededicate his grave; it carries the rough edges of family memory rather than the polish of the archive, but its spine sits inside one of the defining episodes of the Canadian West.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/mciver/mciver-maple-creek-1885.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A prairie river near Maple Creek with a small boat and a Mounted Police post on the bank" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The South Saskatchewan River country near Maple Creek, 1885 &mdash; where George McIver was shot during the North-West Rebellion. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><p>George McIver, born on Lewis around 1856, emigrated young and ranged far &mdash; by the family&rsquo;s account, as far as California. In the spring of 1885 he read that the <strong>North-West Rebellion</strong> had broken out on the Saskatchewan: Louis Riel and the Métis, with Cree and Assiniboine allies, risen against the Dominion around Batoche and Prince Albert. George had brothers settled in that country and started north to reach them &mdash; overland to Fort Calgary, where he built a boat and set out to float down the Bow and the South Saskatchewan. On 23 July 1885, having pulled ashore to cook a meal, he was fired upon from the riverbank, shoved his boat back into the current, and escaped downstream, bleeding badly. He was carried to the North-West Mounted Police post at <strong>Maple Creek</strong>, where he died of the wound on <strong>29 July 1885</strong> &mdash; by the family&rsquo;s account, the first person buried in the Maple Creek cemetery.</p><p>There is a final, aching turn: for more than a century his grave bore the wrong name &mdash; <strong>&ldquo;William McIvor&rdquo;</strong> &mdash; and the family lost the place where he lay. Only in 1989 did they find him again; a new cross was cut with his right name, and the next summer the family rededicated it, closing with a Latin vow: <em>Nunquam obliviscar</em> &mdash; <em>I shall never forget</em>.</p><p>Two honest cautions belong with this. It is family memory committed to paper in 1990, not a contemporary record; the fine details &mdash; above all the identity of whoever fired on him &mdash; are exactly what a century of retelling hardens, and the 1990 text names a Cree chief in a way no contemporary source confirms, so that particular is best left unrepeated. And the account places Donald and Roderick &ldquo;near Prince Albert&rdquo; that year, whereas Donald&rsquo;s documented homestead lies far to the southeast near Saltcoats &mdash; so either Donald was first in the Prince Albert country, or the telling simply blurred the map. The shape, though, is not in doubt: the McIver brothers came out of Lewis into a raw and dangerous country, and one of them did not survive his first Canadian summer, killed in the violent making of the very province his nieces and nephews would help to settle. George left no children. He is a great-great-great-granduncle to Patience, Daniel, and Faith.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-donald-and-lizzie-married-1888-the-marriage-that"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Five</span>Donald and Lizzie (married 1888) &mdash; the marriage that crossed the wall</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">m. 13 Feb 1888 · Earlswood, Saskatchewan · twelve children</p><p>On <strong>13 February 1888</strong>, at <strong>Earlswood, Saskatchewan</strong>, <strong>Donald George McIver of Lewis married Elizabeth Ann &ldquo;Lizzie&rdquo; Campbell of South Uist.</strong> Set down in a line, it can pass for an ordinary frontier wedding. It was not.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/mciver/mciver-saltcoats-sod-homestead.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A sod-and-log homestead on the Saltcoats prairie with a large family posed outside" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A sod-and-log homestead on the Saltcoats prairie &mdash; the kind of place Donald and Lizzie raised their dozen children. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>A McIver met a Campbell. And held.</p></aside><p>Donald was Free Presbyterian, of Lewis &mdash; the most rigorously Protestant culture in Scotland. Lizzie was, by the island geography, Roman Catholic of South Uist &mdash; one of the last redoubts of the old faith. In the islands these two communities lived in settled mutual incomprehension, separated by a confessional boundary that structured everything: who you married, which church bell ordered your week, how you were buried. A Lewis Presbyterian and a Uist Catholic marrying in the old country would have been close to unthinkable &mdash; a scandal in two parishes at once.</p><p>And on the prairie, they crossed it. The honest truth is that the <em>mechanics</em> are unresolved: which congregation hosted it, whether a minister or a priest pronounced the words, whose church the children were christened in. We can write the <em>crossing</em>; we cannot yet write the <em>ceremony</em>, and we will not invent one. What we can say is what the crossing meant: eight thousand kilometres from home, with the old parish structures left behind, two people from across the deepest line their society knew decided it mattered less than the marriage. (Whether the wall was truly there to cross, we cannot be certain &mdash; see the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a>; what <em>is</em> certain is that the line they began was Protestant within a generation.) Either way, their union is a small early foreshadowing of the largest theme in this whole family &mdash; the way that, marriage by marriage, faith after faith and people after people would fold together until, lifetimes later, they all ran in the veins of the same three children.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote"><p><strong>A thread to keep hold of.</strong> The wall Donald and Lizzie crossed was only the first of many this larger family would cross. Far to the east, the Protestant-Irish <strong>Dochertys</strong> were down the Lanarkshire pits; in the Yorkshire wool valleys the <strong>Haistes</strong> and <strong>Rycrofts</strong> were raising families to the mill bell; out of the Dutch polders the <strong>Lakemans</strong> were half a century from Calgary. None of them knew the Hebrideans existed &mdash; but the same long century of landlordism and hunger was working on all of them at once, and their descendants would meet in a single Alberta household a hundred and thirty years on.</p></aside><p>They raised an enormous family &mdash; twelve children the records name, very likely more the records have lost &mdash; and the bare roll is itself a portrait of frontier life in all its fertility and grief:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Norman George McIver</strong>, born 24 November 1889 &mdash; of whom much more in the next chapter; his is the name carved on a war memorial in France.</li>
<li><strong>John Duncan McIver</strong>, born 7 October 1890 &mdash; and dead on 7 February 1891, four months old. An infant lost to a prairie winter.</li>
<li><strong>James Roderick McIver</strong>, born 29 April 1893 &mdash; <strong>the direct ancestor: the grandmother&rsquo;s father</strong> (Chapter Eight). He married Mary Ann Birse and died in Edmonton in 1973.</li>
<li><strong>Catherine McIver</strong> (later Morphy), born 29 July 1895; she lived to 1988 and died back in Saltcoats &mdash; the only child buried near where she was born.</li>
<li><strong>Duncan McLeod McIver</strong>, born 20 May 1897; died 1958, Edmonton.</li>
<li><strong>Henrietta Margaret McIver</strong>, born 24 August 1899; died 1972, New Westminster, BC.</li>
<li><strong>John Alexander McIver</strong>, born 28 May 1902 at Saltcoats; died 1970, Edmonton. (For years the tree mistook <em>him</em> for the grandmother&rsquo;s father; he was in fact her uncle &mdash; see Chapter Eight.)</li>
<li><strong>William Donald McIver</strong> (&ldquo;McIvor&rdquo; in his record), born 4 June 1904; died 1981, Edmonton.</li>
<li><strong>Neil Malcolm McIver</strong>, born 23 September 1906; died 1975, New Westminster.</li>
<li><strong>Walter Gordon McIver</strong>, born 27 December 1908; died 1975, Edmonton.</li>
<li><strong>Elizabeth Ann McIver</strong>, born 9 March 1912 &mdash; a <em>daughter</em> of Donald and Lizzie, and not to be confused with her niece, the grandmother Elizabeth Annie (born 1919); the tree tangled the two for years before the bible set it straight.</li>
<li><strong>Christina Agnes McIver</strong>, born 12 December 1915; she travelled furthest of all, dying in 1976 in Boone, Indiana.</li>
</ul><p>Read that list slowly and you can see the whole shape of the family&rsquo;s century in it. An infant lost in the hardest pioneer years; a son killed in the Great War; and then a long roll of children who survived, grew up, and &mdash; one after another, as the colony failed and the prairie emptied &mdash; <em>left</em>: to Edmonton, again and again, and to British Columbia, and one all the way to the American Midwest. The McIvers came to Saskatchewan to put down roots, and the soil would not let them. So they did what their parents had done in the Hebrides: they crossed the water once more, this time the inland water of distance and disappointment, and went looking for a place that would hold them.</p><p>Donald himself died in <strong>Edmonton on 27 November 1932</strong> &mdash; having lived to see his colony fail, his family scatter west, and one of his sons buried overseas. Lizzie outlived him by a dozen years, dying in <strong>Edmonton on 24 March 1944</strong>, in the middle of another world war. The man from Lochs and the woman from South Uist, who had crossed a confessional wall to marry on the open prairie, lie at the end in the same Alberta city &mdash; their long, improbable union outlasting the colony that brought them together and the islands that drove them out.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-norman-george-mciver-1889-1917-the-one-the-famil"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Six</span>Norman George McIver (1889&ndash;1917) &mdash; the one the family gave to the war</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1889&ndash;1917 · Saltcoats → Villers Station, France</p><p>Every family that lived through 1914 to 1918 was asked, one way or another, to pay. Most lines in this larger family sent sons toward that war and &mdash; by luck, distance, or age &mdash; got them back. One line did not. The McIvers of Saltcoats paid the full price, and the record of it is not a family story softened by retelling but a hard fact set in stone and bronze, verified by the war-graves authorities of two countries.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-line__figure--bw heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/mciver/mciver-46th-battalion-1917.jpg' ); ?>" alt="Canadian infantry of the First World War in a sandbagged trench on the Western Front" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Canadian infantry in the trenches, 1917 &mdash; the Western Front where Private Norman George McIver, 46th Battalion, was killed. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The firmest stone the family stands on is a grave in France.</p></aside><p><strong>Norman George McIver</strong> was the eldest son of Donald and Lizzie, born <strong>24 November 1889</strong> in the early colony years (the family bible&rsquo;s date; his 1916 army attestation gives 1890, but his infant brother John Duncan&rsquo;s October 1890 birth makes the bible&rsquo;s 1889 the sounder reading). He grew up on the Saltcoats prairie, one of that crowd of brothers and sisters, in the Gaelic-inflected, hard-working household his parents had built between Lewis and South Uist. And when the war came, he went.</p><p>He enlisted in the <strong>Canadian Expeditionary Force</strong> &mdash; attesting on <strong>9 February 1916 at Yorkton, Saskatchewan</strong> &mdash; service number <strong>887432</strong>, and was posted to the <strong>46th Battalion (Saskatchewan Regiment)</strong>, a unit that would earn, in the grinding attrition of the Western Front, the grim nickname &ldquo;the Suicide Battalion.&rdquo; Private Norman George McIver, a prairie boy of an island family, went to France to fight in the worst war the world had yet seen. He was killed on <strong>3 June 1917</strong>; the war-graves record gives his age as twenty-six.</p><p>That date places his death in the bloody aftermath of the <strong>Battle of Vimy Ridge</strong> &mdash; the great Canadian assault of April 1917 in which all four divisions of the Canadian Corps fought together for the first time. The fighting did not stop when the ridge was taken; through May and into June the Canadians held and pushed forward against ferocious German shellfire, and it was in that grinding, &ldquo;ordinary&rdquo; trench warfare &mdash; the daily slaughter the famous battles were embedded in &mdash; that Norman George died.</p><p>Here the family corrects its own record, gently but firmly, because getting it right is the only honour left to give him. The tree recorded that he died at &ldquo;Vimy Ridge, Belgium.&rdquo; That is wrong twice over: Vimy is not in Belgium &mdash; it is in <strong>France</strong>, in the Pas-de-Calais near Arras &mdash; and Norman is buried not at the ridge itself but a few kilometres away, at <strong>Villers Station Cemetery</strong>, near Villers-au-Bois, about eleven kilometres northwest of Arras. His grave reference is <strong>VIII. C. 12.</strong> The Commonwealth War Graves Commission and the Canadian Virtual War Memorial both hold his record, and both name him <em>Private Norman George McIver, 887432, 46th Battalion, Saskatchewan Regiment, son of Donald and Elizabeth Campbell McIver, of Saltcoats, Saskatchewan, killed in action 3 June 1917.</em></p><p>That parental line is its own small miracle of the record. It means that in the permanent memory of the nation he died for, the names of the man from Lewis and the woman from South Uist stand together &mdash; husband and wife, Presbyterian and Catholic, their improbable prairie marriage attested forever by the war that took their son. It is the single most solidly documented fact in this entire family story, and a heartbreaking one: the firmest stone the family stands on is a grave in France.</p><p>A boy whose grandparents had been cleared off Hebridean crofts by sheep and famine; whose parents had crossed an ocean and a confessional wall to break new ground on the Saskatchewan plain; who was born under a prairie sky into a colony already failing &mdash; that boy lies now in a war cemetery in northern France, under one of the rows of identical white stones that mark where a generation of the world&rsquo;s young men was spent. He has no descendants. He is a great-great-great-granduncle to the children this story is for. But he is the family&rsquo;s witness &mdash; the proof that this line did not merely survive history but was wounded by it. Patience, Daniel, and Faith should know his name: <strong>Norman George McIver.</strong> Three June, 1917. Villers Station. Grave VIII. C. 12.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-the-old-islanders-laid-down-in-prairie-ground"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Seven</span>The old islanders, laid down in prairie ground</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1832&ndash;1920 · the old islanders, laid down at Earlswood</p><p>While Donald and Lizzie&rsquo;s generation was raising its children and sending one to the war, the oldest islanders &mdash; Lizzie&rsquo;s own parents &mdash; were finishing their long lives an ocean from where they began.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--left"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/mciver/mciver-south-uist-cathcart.jpg' ); ?>" alt="A first prairie camp of Hebridean settlers with tents, an ox-cart, and a half-built sod shanty" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A first prairie camp of Hebridean settlers near Wapella &mdash; the Cathcart colony the Campbells came into. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><p><strong>James Alexander Campbell</strong>, the old Catholic of South Uist, lived out his years in the <strong>Wapella</strong> district &mdash; the heart of the old Cathcart colony, exactly where a South Uist Catholic of his cohort should be. He died there on <strong>31 March 1920</strong>, aged eighty-seven. Pause over that span: born the year before the British Empire abolished slavery, dead two years after the Armistice that ended the war that killed his grandson Norman. He had seen the famine, the clearances, the emigrant ship, the railway, the breaking of the prairie, and a death by machine-gun and shellfire of a kind no one on South Uist in 1832 could have dreamed. He is buried at <strong>Earlswood Cemetery</strong>, in the Rural Municipality of Martin No. 122, about eighteen kilometres south of Wapella &mdash; and here again the family corrects its tree. An earlier version placed a Campbell burial at Kelso, in RM Walpole. That is wrong: the Campbell graves are at <strong>Earlswood</strong>, where the transcription records a whole cluster of them &mdash; James himself; his son <strong>Neil Alexander Campbell</strong>; another son, <strong>Duncan Campbell</strong> &mdash; the family of South Uist gathered together under prairie sod.</p><p>And <strong>Catherine Cameron</strong> &mdash; James&rsquo;s wife, the woman from the Spey-mouth of Moray &mdash; also crossed the water and died in the Wapella country, the family record holds. There is an unresolved thread the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a> take up honestly: a &ldquo;Kate Campbell, wife of James,&rdquo; lies in that same Earlswood cemetery, died 1890, and whether that is Catherine herself (with the family birth-year slightly off) or another woman is a question the records have not yet settled. What is not in doubt is that the Cameron&ndash;Campbell marriage produced the line that runs forward &mdash; through their daughter Lizzie, who married Donald McIver &mdash; and that the old Moray-and-Uist couple ended their crossing where the McIvers and Campbells ended theirs: in soil that no factor could clear them from, because at last it was theirs.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-james-roderick-mciver-and-mary-ann-birse-the-cha"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Eight</span>James Roderick McIver and Mary Ann Birse &mdash; the chair that was empty, and is now filled</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1893&ndash;2002 · James Roderick McIver &amp; Mary Ann Birse</p><p>Now we come to the generation through which everything passes &mdash; and to the most satisfying turn in the making of this story: a chair that sat empty through its first telling, and has since been filled.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>The chair is no longer empty.</p></aside><p>When this story was first written, the record went quiet exactly here. The family tree said Thomas&rsquo;s grandmother, Elizabeth Annie McIver, was the daughter of <strong>John Alexander McIver</strong> and an unnamed wife &mdash; and that wife, the direct great-grandmother of the line, had no name anywhere in the record. There was an empty chair at the table of this generation, a blank where a whole person belonged. This story refused to fill it with a guess; it left the chair empty, pointed to where she should be, and promised to keep looking.</p><p>Then the looking paid off &mdash; through a set of family records held by Thomas&rsquo;s uncle <strong>David Docherty</strong>: the McIver family bible, and a painstaking genealogy compiled by the wider McIver clan. Together they filled the chair, and revealed that it had been set in the wrong place all along. For the tree had the wrong father. Elizabeth Annie was <strong>not</strong> the daughter of John Alexander McIver. She was the daughter of his older brother, <strong>James Roderick McIver</strong> &mdash; and the arithmetic alone should have warned us. John Alexander, born in 1902, would have been just seventeen when Elizabeth Annie was born in 1919; James Roderick, born at Saltcoats on <strong>29 April 1893</strong>, was twenty-six &mdash; exactly the age to be a father. The two were brothers, both sons of Donald and Lizzie, and somewhere in the long assembling of the tree one had been quietly written in for the other.</p><p>And the woman in the chair &mdash; the great-grandmother &mdash; was <strong>Mary Ann Birse.</strong> She had been in the family&rsquo;s records the whole time, mislabelled merely an &ldquo;aunt&rdquo; when she was in truth the direct great-grandmother. She was born at Saltcoats on <strong>12 August 1900</strong>, married James Roderick on <strong>15 August 1917</strong> (the entry sits in the McIver bible&rsquo;s own marriage register), and outlived very nearly everyone else in this story: she died in <strong>Edmonton on 28 October 2002, at the age of one hundred and two.</strong> A woman born into the failing Hebridean colony on the Saskatchewan prairie, in the reign of Queen Victoria, lived to see the twenty-first century &mdash; and at her funeral, her grandson David Docherty was a pallbearer.</p><p>James Roderick and Mary made the family&rsquo;s second great crossing &mdash; the drift from the failing colony land to <strong>Edmonton.</strong> Their eldest, <strong>Jonathan Roderick McIver</strong>, was born in 1918 and died the same year, an infant lost. Then came <strong>Elizabeth Anne &mdash; the grandmother &mdash; in 1919.</strong> Then <strong>Eleanor Mary Jane</strong> in 1921, who married into the Golden family and became, across her own long life, the keeper of the family&rsquo;s dates and stories: it was &ldquo;Aunty Eleanor&rdquo; whose careful lists, decades later, supply so much of what this very story is built on. The woman in the once-empty chair raised the family&rsquo;s own historian.</p><p><strong>John Alexander McIver</strong> &mdash; the brother the tree mistook for the father &mdash; keeps his honest place in the record, just not this one. He was born at Saltcoats on <strong>28 May 1902</strong> (registration #1831 names his parents as Donald G. McIver and Lizzie Campbell &mdash; a third independent attestation of the prairie marriage); he married <strong>Mary &ldquo;Donna&rdquo; Coleman</strong> in Edmonton in 1944 and died there on <strong>30 December 1970.</strong> (That date is itself a correction: an earlier tree had him dying in 1940 at Saltcoats &mdash; a misreading of a cemetery stone for a different, younger John McIver.) He was a great-grand-<em>uncle</em> of the line, not its great-grandfather.</p><p>One gold-standard confirmation remains available &mdash; Elizabeth Annie&rsquo;s own <strong>1919 Saskatchewan birth registration</strong>, which names James Roderick and Mary outright, and which is now past its hundred-year seal. But the chair is no longer empty. It holds a woman named <strong>Mary Ann Birse</strong>, who lived a hundred and two years, and beside her <strong>James Roderick McIver</strong>, her husband and the grandmother&rsquo;s father. <em>Buanseasmhachd</em> cuts both ways: it is what kept the chair honestly empty until the truth could come and fill it.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-elizabeth-annie-mciver-1919-1988-the-grandmother"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">Chapter Nine</span>Elizabeth Annie McIver (1919&ndash;1988) &mdash; the grandmother who carried the islands into the family</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">1919&ndash;1988 · Saltcoats → Alberta · the islands come ashore</p><p><strong>Elizabeth Annie McIver</strong> &mdash; the daughter of <strong>James Roderick McIver and Mary Ann Birse</strong> &mdash; was born at <strong>Saltcoats, Saskatchewan, on 20 March 1919</strong>, in the first spring after the Great War, two years after her uncle Norman George had been buried in France. She was baptized that spring &mdash; on <strong>27 April 1919, at Perley, Saskatchewan</strong>, by the Rev. C. Murdoch &mdash; and the small detail does quiet work, for a Presbyterian baptism places this McIver&ndash;Campbell family, only a generation on from the islands, firmly inside the <strong>Protestant</strong> church (see the <a href="#lr-notes">Notes</a> on the religion question). She was the last of this family to be born on the Saskatchewan prairie the islanders had broken; in her, the long Hebridean stream finally turns toward Alberta for good.</p><figure class="heritage-line__figure heritage-longread__figure--right"><img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_stylesheet_directory_uri() . '/assets/img/heritage/mciver/mciver-edmonton-terminus.jpg' ); ?>" alt="An Edmonton street in the 1920s with brick storefronts, early automobiles, and a streetcar" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An Edmonton street in the 1920s &mdash; the city that became the McIvers&rsquo; true terminus. <em>(Illustrative.)</em></figcaption></figure><p>For she, too, made the family&rsquo;s characteristic crossing &mdash; not over an ocean this time, but over the distance of a young country, from the failing colony land into the same Edmonton that had drawn so many of her aunts and uncles. And it was in central Alberta that the Hebridean line met the Irish one, when Elizabeth Annie McIver married <strong>Thomas Richard Docherty</strong> &mdash; the wedding held at the Docherty home at Alix on <strong>15 August 1941.</strong> Thomas Richard was born in 1914 at Alix, the son of a line that ran back through the coal-and-iron towns of industrial Scotland to a Protestant-Irish emigration out of Donegal (that line&rsquo;s story tells itself elsewhere). When they married, two of the great migrant streams of the British Isles ran together: the cleared Gaels of the Hebrides and the Ulster-Irish out of Donegal, both driven from their homelands by the same long century of landlordism and hardship, both fetching up at last in Alberta.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote"><p><strong>The convergence begins here.</strong> This marriage is one half of a meeting the whole larger family turns on &mdash; and the Docherty story tells the same moment from its own side. Elizabeth Annie McIver, with Lewis and South Uist and Moray behind her, and Thomas Richard Docherty, with Donegal and Lanarkshire behind him, were two of the great migrant streams of the British Isles running at last into one channel. Their daughter would carry both inheritances to a son born in Calgary &mdash; the very city where, from a third direction, out of the Dutch Indies by way of a long war, the <strong>Lakeman</strong> line had also come to rest. Three rivers, one prairie city.</p></aside><p>Elizabeth Annie lived a long Alberta life and died in <strong>Edmonton on 28 April 1988</strong> &mdash; in the same city where her father, her grandfather Donald, and her grandmother Lizzie had all died before her, the city that had become the McIvers&rsquo; true terminus after the islands and the prairie. She is the <strong>maternal grandmother</strong> of Thomas Cheesman &mdash; close enough to living memory that she is not a figure out of the archives but a real grandmother, remembered. Through her, every stream in this story &mdash; Lewis and South Uist and Moray, McIver and Campbell and Cameron, the Gaelic psalm and the <em>machair</em> and the moor &mdash; flowed down into her daughter, and from her daughter into Thomas.</p><p>Her daughter &mdash; Thomas&rsquo;s mother &mdash; is <strong>Elizabeth Maryanne</strong>, born in 1959. With her the story crosses out of history and into living family, and so, in keeping with this family&rsquo;s care for the privacy of the people still alive in it, the narrative does not linger over her or the children&rsquo;s other living relations. It is enough to say that Elizabeth Maryanne carried the whole Hebridean inheritance &mdash; her own mother&rsquo;s islands, her own father&rsquo;s Ireland &mdash; forward one more generation, to a son born in Calgary. And there the Hebridean stream meets the rest of the rivers.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-where-the-hebridean-rivers-join-the-others"><span class="heritage-longread__eyebrow">The Living Line</span>Where the Hebridean rivers join the others</h2><p class="heritage-longread__kicker">Grande Prairie · where the Hebridean rivers join the others</p><p>Every long family story has a moment when it stops being history and becomes the present. For the McIvers and the Campbells and the Camerons &mdash; for the whole Hebridean stream that began on three islands and a glen before any of these dates were written down &mdash; that moment is now.</p><aside class="heritage-line__quote heritage-longread__pq" aria-hidden="true"><p>They are the far shore. They are what buanseasmhachd was for.</p></aside><p>The current runs down through the grandmother, <strong>Elizabeth Annie McIver</strong>, into her daughter <strong>Elizabeth Maryanne</strong>, and from her into <strong>Thomas Cheesman</strong>, born in Calgary on the fourth of November, 1980. Thomas carries the islands in his blood: Lewis and South Uist and Moray, the McIver and the Campbell and the Cameron, the Free Presbyterian moor and the Catholic <em>machair</em>, the marriage that crossed the confessional wall on the open prairie. He also carries, through his mother, the <strong>Docherty</strong> line &mdash; the famine-Irish current out of Donegal and industrial Lanarkshire that ran together with the McIver line when Elizabeth Annie married Thomas Richard Docherty. And through his father, <strong>Martin Gerard Lakeman</strong>, born in Calgary in 1957, he carries the <strong>Lakeman</strong> line &mdash; three centuries of Dutch polder and the Indies and a long sea-going history of its own. The name he himself bears and passes on, <strong>Cheesman</strong>, is a chosen channel rather than a blood one: the surname of his step-father&rsquo;s family, taken not by descent but by belonging.</p><p>Thomas and <strong>Melanie Haiste</strong> &mdash; born in Grande Prairie in 1983, herself the meeting-place of the <strong>Haiste</strong> line out of Yorkshire and Saskatchewan and the <strong>Rycroft</strong> and <strong>Steinke</strong> lines out of Leeds and Hawai&rsquo;i and the Peace Country &mdash; married in Calgary on the second of July, 2016. They are the co-parents of three children, raised in Grande Prairie:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Patience Kristie-Anne Cheesman</strong>, born 24 September 2013;</li>
<li><strong>Daniel Eric Thomas Cheesman</strong>, born 30 June 2015;</li>
<li><strong>Faith Lana Lyn Cheesman</strong>, born 30 March 2017.</li>
</ul><p>These three inherit it all, together, as a sibling-group &mdash; not one heir but three, each carrying the whole. The Hebridean lines reach them through their father, by way of his grandmother Elizabeth Annie McIver: the islands come ashore in Patience, in Daniel, and in Faith. And there is a quiet rhyme worth naming for them one day. Their father&rsquo;s Hebridean ancestors broke prairie soil in southeastern Saskatchewan in the 1880s &mdash; the McIvers near Saltcoats, the Campbells near Wapella &mdash; a full generation before their mother&rsquo;s Haiste ancestors would break Saskatchewan soil of their own around 1907, and both families, having tried the prairie, pushed on west to Alberta. Two crossings of the same dust, made decades apart by people who would never meet, until their descendants married a hundred and thirty years later.</p><p>None of the islanders could have imagined the world these three children are born into. Donald McIver of the Lochs of Lewis, singing the Gaelic psalm in a turf house on a failing colony; Lizzie Campbell of South Uist, carrying her faith across a Protestant ocean; old James Alexander Campbell, born the year before the Empire freed its slaves and dead two years after the Armistice; Catherine Cameron of the Spey-mouth, whose very name we hold honestly and lightly because the parchment that proves her has not yet been found; Norman George, the boy they gave to the war, under his white stone in France &mdash; not one of them could have pictured an Alberta city, a marriage in Calgary, three children in Grande Prairie in whom the moor and the <em>machair</em>, the psalm and the Mass, the cleared and the famished and the chosen, all run together at last in a single household.</p><p>There is that word the islands carried long before they ever saw a prairie &mdash; <em>buanseasmhachd</em>, the holding of ground when the ground itself is pulled away. The McIvers and the Campbells and the Camerons spent it in full. They were cleared off three homelands and they held. They crossed the water &mdash; <em>a&rsquo; dol thar a&rsquo; chuain</em>, the hardest journey of all &mdash; and they held. They lost a son to a war, and a colony to the climate, and the name of a great-grandmother to the silence of the record, and still &mdash; still &mdash; something came through. Patience, Daniel, and Faith inherit it together. The world they will live through is not yet written. But the family has been here before, on three islands and a glen and a hard prairie, and it crossed the water, and it held its ground, and it came ashore. They are the far shore. They are what <em>buanseasmhachd</em> was for.</p><h2 class="heritage-longread__chapter" id="lr-lineage-at-a-glance">Lineage at a glance</h2><div class="heritage-longread__table-wrap"><table class="heritage-longread__table">
