</table></div><p><strong>Confidence tiers:</strong> <em>Verified</em> &mdash; anchored to at least one primary register or government record (parish register, civil registration, census, CWGC, homestead file). <em>Probable</em> &mdash; rests on indexed family-tree compilations consistent across sources but lacking direct primary-register confirmation. <em>Inherited</em> &mdash; rests on the family genealogy without independent primary verification. <em>Living memory</em> &mdash; recent enough to be remembered directly by family.</p><div class="heritage-longread__ribbon" aria-hidden="true"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Neil McIver</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">c. 1779</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Norman MacIver</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">c. 1811&ndash;1893</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Donald George</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1850&ndash;1932</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">James Roderick</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1893&ndash;1973</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Elizabeth Annie</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">1919&ndash;1988</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Elizabeth Maryanne</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">b. 1959</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Thomas</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">b. 1980</span></span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-node"><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-name">Patience · Daniel · Faith</span><span class="heritage-longread__ribbon-dates">2013&ndash;2017</span></span></div><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>Side-branches named in the records but off the direct line: Donald&rsquo;s brothers <strong>George McIver</strong> (1856&ndash;1885), killed in the North-West Rebellion and the first burial at Maple Creek (see the Interlude), and <strong>Roderick Angus McIver</strong>; the eleven brothers and sisters of James Roderick named in Chapter Five; the Campbell sons <strong>Neil Alexander</strong> and <strong>Duncan</strong> of the Earlswood cluster; and <strong>Kenneth McIver</strong> (1836&ndash;1933) of Saltcoats, whose exact link to Donald is still unresolved.</em></p><details class="heritage-longread__notes" id="lr-notes">
<summary>Notes on the records</summary>
<div class="heritage-longread__notes-body">
<p>This branch of the family is, unusually, <strong>well supported by primary records</strong> &mdash; far better than the paternal Docherty line, which rests largely on tree-assertions about pre-Famine Ireland. The McIver/Campbell/Cameron stream can be written with real confidence, precisely <em>because</em> its strongest facts come not from family lore but from government registers, census enumerations, cemetery transcriptions, and war-graves records. But confidence is not completeness, and several threads are tagged honestly here so no reader mistakes a plausible inference for a proven fact.</p><p><strong>The McIver family bible &mdash; a key primary source.</strong> A set of photographs of the <strong>McIver family bible</strong> and related papers, held by Thomas&rsquo;s uncle <strong>David Docherty</strong>, materially strengthens the record. The bible&rsquo;s handwritten <strong>births, marriages, and deaths registers</strong> &mdash; contemporaneous family entries &mdash; confirm the sibling list of Donald and Lizzie&rsquo;s children and several dates the story had taken from the tree, and settle one tree-builder error for good: the *<em>Elizabeth Ann born 9 March 1912 is recorded plainly as a </em>daughter<em> of Donald and Lizzie</em><em> &mdash; a sibling, not (as the old data had wrongly slotted her) the grandmother or John Alexander&rsquo;s wife. The deaths register gives </em><em>Donald&rsquo;s death as 27 November 1932</em><em>, a month earlier than the compiled tree&rsquo;s December date; the register, written nearer the event, is the better authority. Two </em><em>funeral cards</em><em> in the same collection &mdash; Elizabeth Annie&rsquo;s (1988, Eastminster Presbyterian) and Mary Ann McIver&rsquo;s (2002, Mill Woods Presbyterian) &mdash; anchor the family&rsquo;s Protestant church life. And, most consequentially, the </em><em>compiled McIver genealogy in the same collection cracked the missing-great-grandmother puzzle</em><em>: it shows Elizabeth Annie as the daughter of </em><em>James Roderick McIver</em><em> and </em><em>Mary Ann Birse</em>*, not John Alexander.</p><p><strong>1. The verified bedrock.</strong></p><ul>
<li><strong>Norman George McIver&rsquo;s war death.</strong> Service #887432, Private, 46th Battalion CEF, killed 3 June 1917, buried Villers Station Cemetery, France, grave VIII.C.12 &mdash; held by both the Commonwealth War Graves Commission and the Canadian Virtual War Memorial (entry 59994). His CEF personnel file (LAC Item ID 164000) gives birth 24 Nov 1890 and enlistment 9 Feb 1916 at Yorkton; the CVWM independently names his parents as <strong>Donald and Elizabeth Campbell McIver of Saltcoats</strong> &mdash; a priceless second attestation of the marriage, independent of the family tree. <em>(On the birth year: the bible&rsquo;s 24 Nov 1889 is preferred here, since brother John Duncan&rsquo;s October 1890 birth rules out a late-1890 Norman; the attestation&rsquo;s &ldquo;1890&rdquo; is the kind of slip common on enlistment papers.)</em></li>
<li><strong>John Alexander McIver&rsquo;s birth.</strong> SK birth registration <strong>#1831</strong>, born 28 May 1902 at Saltcoats, mother <strong>Lizzie Campbell</strong>, father <strong>Donald G McIver</strong> &mdash; a third independent confirmation of the parents&rsquo; union.</li>
<li><strong>Donald&rsquo;s Saltcoats homestead</strong>, catalogued in the Saskatchewan Archives pre-1930 homestead records as <strong>NE 36-24-01-W2</strong> (ref. S 42.687816, confirmed against the live PAS catalogue), with a second file at W 6-13-01-W2 (S 42.462861). Both are order-only; the homestead file would give Donald&rsquo;s island of origin and arrival year.</li>
</ul><p><strong>2. Corrections carried into the narrative.</strong></p><ul>
<li><strong>Norman George died in FRANCE, not &ldquo;Vimy Ridge, Belgium.&rdquo;</strong> Vimy is in France; his burial is at Villers Station Cemetery, ~11 km NW of Arras.</li>
<li><strong>John Alexander McIver died 30 December 1970 in EDMONTON, not November 1940 in Saltcoats.</strong> The &ldquo;1940 Saltcoats&rdquo; entry is a misreading of a Saltcoats stone for a different, younger John McIver.</li>
<li><strong>The Campbell cluster is buried at EARLSWOOD Cemetery</strong>, RM Martin No. 122 &mdash; <strong>not</strong> at Kelso / RM Walpole as the tree had it.</li>
<li><strong>The Lothian Colony near Saltcoats is dated 1889</strong>; Donald immigrated in <strong>1883</strong> and so predated it &mdash; an early independent Lewis emigrant, not a Lothian settler. (An earlier draft mis-dated the colony to &ldquo;1884&ndash;85.&rdquo;)</li>
<li><strong>The great-grandmother&rsquo;s maiden name is BIRSE, not &ldquo;Rodgers.&rdquo;</strong> Aunty Eleanor&rsquo;s data sheets called her &ldquo;Mary Rodgers&rdquo;; the primary SK birth registration (#3985, naming Elizabeth Annie&rsquo;s mother) settles it as <strong>Mary Ann Birse</strong>.</li>
</ul><p>*<em>3. The settlement schemes &mdash; held as </em>probable<em>.</em><em> The two emigrant fleets are documented facts; the family&rsquo;s </em>placement<em> in them is a strong but unproven inference. The </em><em>Campbells of South Uist</em><em> fit the </em><em>Cathcart Colony (1883&ndash;84)</em><em> to Wapella/Moosomin on island, years, and destination; a local history (J. N. MacKinnon, </em>A Short History of the Pioneer Scotch Settlers of St Andrew&rsquo;s<em>, 1922) and aggregated trees point to a Campbell arrival at Quebec in </em><em>May 1884</em><em> with Cathcart&rsquo;s second contingent &mdash; a promising lead, not yet nailed to a manifest. The </em><em>McIvers of Lewis</em><em> settled in the Saltcoats district later organised around the Lothian Colony (1889), but Donald arrived in </em><em>1883</em>*, ahead of it.</p><p><strong>4. The marriage, and the religion question.</strong> That Donald of Lewis married Lizzie Campbell of South Uist is <em>attested three times over</em> &mdash; the CVWM parental line on Norman George&rsquo;s war record, John Alexander&rsquo;s birth registration, and the consistent family record. What is <strong>not</strong> attested is the couple&rsquo;s <em>religions</em>: the &ldquo;Free Presbyterian × Roman Catholic&rdquo; framing is an inference from island geography (Lewis Protestant, South Uist Catholic), not a fact those records establish. And the family&rsquo;s later record cuts against the dramatic version &mdash; the children and grandchildren were <strong>Protestant</strong> (Elizabeth Annie&rsquo;s 1988 funeral at Eastminster Presbyterian; Mary Ann&rsquo;s 2002 at Mill Woods Presbyterian; the extended family&rsquo;s weddings ran Presbyterian, Baptist, Anglican, Lutheran, United &mdash; never Catholic). So either Lizzie came from South Uist&rsquo;s Protestant minority, or (if Catholic-born) the family assimilated wholly to Presbyterianism in Canada. The cross-confessional marriage remains <em>possible</em>, even likely on the geography &mdash; but it is a hypothesis, not documented drama.</p><p>*<em>5. Catherine Cameron &mdash; the </em>probable<em> great-great-great-grandmother.</em><em> Her birth (~1844), her Moray origin (Urquhart / Spey Bay, OPR parish #144), and her marriage to James Alexander Campbell all come from the family tree and are </em>consistent<em> with the records but </em><em>not yet retrieved from a primary source.</em><em> Her baptism, if the date is right, should lie in OPR #144. There is also an </em><em>unresolved death question</em><em>: a &ldquo;Kate Campbell, wife of James,&rdquo; died 24 August 1890 aged 43 and is buried at Earlswood &mdash; she may be Catherine (with the birth-year off by a few years) or an earlier wife. A newer wrinkle sharpens it: an </em><em>1866</em><em> marriage date for James and Catherine has surfaced in aggregated trees, which would </em>post-date<em> Lizzie&rsquo;s c. 1860 birth &mdash; raising the possibility that Lizzie was the daughter of an </em>earlier* wife of James, or that one of the dates is simply wrong. The 1866 marriage record and Lizzie&rsquo;s own baptism would resolve it.</p><p><strong>6. The great-grandmother &mdash; FOUND, confirmed by primary record.</strong> This was, in the first draft, the single largest gap. A MyHeritage search of the Saskatchewan Births 1832&ndash;1921 collection (30 May 2026) returned <strong>SK birth reg #3985</strong> (Elizabeth Annie McIver, b. 20 March 1919 &mdash; father <strong>James R. McIver</strong>, mother <strong>Mary Ann Birse</strong>) and <strong>SK birth reg #979</strong> (James Rodrick McIver, b. 29 April 1893 &mdash; father <strong>Donald G. McIver</strong>, mother <strong>Lizzie Campbell</strong>). Both parentage rows are now <em>Verified</em>, and the wrong-father and &ldquo;Mary Rodgers&rdquo; errors are corrected.</p><p><strong>7. Donald George McIver &mdash; origin and parents.</strong> A FamilySearch Family Tree record (retrieved 30 May 2026) gives birth <strong>1850, Lochs</strong> (corrected from the compiled tree&rsquo;s ~1856); residence 1881 at <strong>Carloway</strong>; immigration <strong>1883</strong>; marriage <strong>13 February 1888, Earlswood</strong>; parents <strong>Norman MacIver + Henrietta / Effie / Helen MacIver (born MacAulay)</strong> &mdash; family GEDCOMs vary her given name and one records her death in 1897; and siblings including <strong>Roderick Angus McIver</strong> and <strong>George McIver</strong> (the 1885 Rebellion casualty). This is a compiled tree, treated as <strong>Probable</strong> for birth year, parents, and immigration year pending the PAS homestead file or ScotlandsPeople records &mdash; but its detail is consistent with every primary record we hold. (The <strong>1891 Scotland Census Donald McIver of Barvas/Arnol</strong> is a different man; ours had been in Canada since 1883.)</p><p>*<em>8. What the </em>next<em> research should do.</em><em> In rough order of value: order the </em><em>PAS homestead file for NE 36-24-01-W2</em><em> (S 42.687816) to confirm Donald&rsquo;s island of origin and arrival; retrieve </em><em>ScotlandsPeople</em><em> for Catherine Cameron&rsquo;s ~1844 baptism (OPR #144), the 1866 Campbell&ndash;Cameron marriage, Lizzie&rsquo;s birth, and the deep Lewis (Lochs / Free Church) McIver generations; obtain the full </em><em>Earlswood Cemetery transcription</em><em> to resolve the &ldquo;Kate Campbell 1890&rdquo; identity; and pull </em><em>Mary Ann Birse&rsquo;s</em>* own 1900 birth registration and parents.</p><p class="heritage-longread__aside"><em>This is a living document. As the records give up more of what they hold &mdash; and on this branch, unusually, they still have a great deal to give &mdash; it will be revised. The aim is not a finished monument but a faithful one: confident where the evidence is strong, honest where it is thin, and never willing to invent a parent, a parish, or a date to make a sentence read more smoothly than the truth allows.</em></p>
</div>
